// The common input to loop operations. `Range(start, end)` is a concrete
// half-open index interval, while `<R: Range<T>>` accepts any type that
// supplies the same bounds and indexed access. Implementations live beside
// their owning types to preserve the dependency direction of std.core.

Res = std.core.result

// A half-open index interval [start, end), plus what lives at an
// index. `at` is the one operation an impl supplies; None ends the walk
// early, so a container whose length moved under the loop stops rather
// than reading past itself. Optional hook (`::=`, no body): supplying
// none walks the index space itself — `Range(0, v.len)` is the
// ordinary way to walk indices (how std.collections uses it).
Range*<T> = {
    start*: usize,
    end*: usize,

    at* ::= (self: @Self, index: usize) Res<T>
}

// Vec implements Range beside Vec and forwards `at` to get. Its bounds are
// supplied values rather than stored fields, so a loop observes a changed
// length before its next iteration. Fixed arrays receive their implementation
// from the compiler because an array type constructor has no owning module.
